[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Each of these is a point where the C does something surprising and where a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m plausible Rust translation would diverge. All were confirmed identical.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. `fgets` does not read across newlines
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `fgets(in, sizeof(in), stdin)` reads one line only; the rest of stdin is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m discarded. A translation using `read_to_string` or `scanf`-like semantics would
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m differ on `"3\n7\n"` — the C parses `3` and never sees the `7`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by `fgets_stops_at_first_newline`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. The buffer is 100 bytes, so input is cut at 99
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `in` is `char[100]`, so at most 99 bytes are read and the newline may never
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m arrive. A number straddling that boundary is silently truncated: 98 spaces
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m followed by `12345` parses as **1**, not 12345, because only the first digit
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m fits. `translation/src/main.rs` reproduces this with `fgets(100)` reading while
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `out.len() + 1 < size`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by `fgets_99_byte_truncation`, including newlines landing at bytes 99,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 100 and 101.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 3. The buffer is consumed as a C string, so an embedded NUL ends it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `char in[100] = ""` zero-fills, and `strtol` stops at the first NUL. Input
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `"\0" + "12"` therefore parses nothing and prints the error, even though `fgets`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m did read the digits into the buffer. The Rust truncates `raw` at the first NUL
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m byte before parsing, which is what makes this match.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by `embedded_nul_terminates_the_c_string`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 4. Empty stdin leaves the buffer valid, not undefined
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m On immediate EOF `fgets` returns NULL and does not touch `in`, which is still
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `""` from its initialiser. `strtol("")` converts nothing, `endp == str`, so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program prints `An error occurred` and still returns **0**. The Rust returns an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m empty `Vec` from its `fgets`, reaching the same path.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by `empty_and_eof_input`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 5. Trailing garbage is accepted
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `parse_val` only requires `endp != str` — that at least one digit converted. It
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m never checks that the whole string was consumed. So `"12abc"` succeeds with 12,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `"2.9"` succeeds with 2, and `"0x10"` succeeds with 0 (base 10 stops at `x`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A translation using Rust's `str::parse::<i32>()` would reject all three.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by `trailing_garbage_is_accepted`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 6. Rejection has two distinct causes that must not be conflated
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Out of `int` range but in `long` range** — `"2147483648"`, `"9223372036854775807"`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `strtol` succeeds and sets no errno; the `tmp <= INT_MAX` guard rejects.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Out of `long` range** — `"9223372036854775808"`: `strtol` saturates to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `LONG_MAX` *and* sets `ERANGE`; the `errno == 0` guard rejects.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Both print the same message, so a test checking only stdout cannot tell them
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m apart — but a translation that clamped instead of failing, or that failed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m instead of clamping, would break on one side. `"-9223372036854775808"` is the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m delicate case: it is exactly `LONG_MIN`, converts with **no** `ERANGE`, and is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m rejected purely by the `tmp >= INT_MIN` guard. The Rust `strtol_base10` uses a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m separate magnitude limit of `i64::MAX as u64 + 1` when negative to get this
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m right.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by `int_range_boundaries`, `long_range_boundaries_and_erange`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `every_int_boundary_value_exhaustively`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 7. Leading zeros do not cause overflow
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 80 zeros followed by `2147483647` is accepted, because `strtol` tracks the value
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and not the digit count. Any translation that pre-checked input length would
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m wrongly reject it.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by `leading_zeros_do_not_trigger_overflow`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 8. Signed `int` overflow in `add_bedrooms`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `bedrooms` starts at 5, so `5 + INT_MAX` overflows. This is undefined behaviour
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m in C; gcc wraps in practice. Confirmed that `-O0` and `-O3` builds of the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m produce identical output here, and that both match the Rust, which uses
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `wrapping_add` deliberately. Because `run()` is called twice, the value wraps
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and then wraps back — `2147483647` gives bedrooms `-2147483644` then `3`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by `global_state_persists_across_both_runs`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 9. Global state persists between the two `run()` calls
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `the_house` is a mutable global, so the second `run()` continues from the first:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m floors go 2→4 and bathrooms 2.5→4.5 across the eight printed lines. A
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m translation that reinitialised state per call would produce eight lines of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m plausible-looking but wrong output.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by `output_shape_is_exactly_what_the_c_prints`, which pins the exact
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m expected bytes.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 10. `%.1f` formatting
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `bathrooms` only ever takes the values 2.5, 3.5 and 4.5. All three are exactly
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m representable, so glibc's round-half-to-even and Rust's `{:.1}` cannot disagree,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and the locale decimal separator is not a factor because the C never calls
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `setlocale`. The NaN/infinity arms of `format_f1` in `src/main.rs` are therefore
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m unreachable dead code, kept only for faithfulness.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 11. Whitespace, and what is not whitespace
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `strtol` skips leading whitespace per the C locale — space, tab, newline,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m vertical tab, form feed, CR. Bytes `0xFF`/`0xFE` are **not** whitespace and are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m rejected. A leading `\n` is especially easy to get wrong: it is skipped by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `strtol`, but `fgets` already stopped at it, so `"\n5\n"` never sees the `5` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m errors.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by `leading_whitespace_is_skipped`, `no_conversion_is_an_error`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 12. Exit status is unconditionally 0, and stderr is always empty
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main` ends in `return 0` on both paths, and the error message goes to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **stdout** via `printf`, not stderr. A translation that used `eprintln!` or
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `std::process::exit(1)` for the error path would produce identical-looking
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m terminal output while failing both the stderr and exit-status comparisons.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by `errors_go_to_stdout_not_stderr_and_exit_is_always_zero`.
